<p>Taiwanese President Lai Ching-te urged the Trump administration on Sunday to keep selling weapons to Taiwan, saying U.S. arms sales and security cooperation are “key elements” in maintaining regional peace and stability. Lai’s statement followed President Trump’s comments after a two-day summit with Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing that he had not approved a pending $14 billion arms sale to Taiwan and might use it as a “negotiating chip” with China. Trump told Fox News the decision “depends on China.” U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said Sunday there was “no change” in American policy on Taiwan and that Trump had made no commitments to Xi on Taiwan. Taiwan says China’s military pressure is the main threat to stability. Beijing claims the self-governing island as Chinese territory and has not ruled out using force to bring it under its control. Lai said Taiwan “will never be sacrificed or traded away” and would not give up its democratic way of life under pressure.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/d-1yeX78c8Tyw21eW0omhZq20kOZ3lrojdu2s2beUk-hsCYWFvgEYhjn7DyqWnK_i0kUecWDExZZjeOCM6cFeks06SI-uXlX_Hs3k1ZIqwYZ_YNIiosjfmpV87INaBLicg5nVe0sjO9dsNvxMfiY0XaqfXrYNy7gQE417jZPYeCuR1y7WmCZx57n6m2RYLwEPgS1KxOv_EBKvHGyapmc1eFYxgCscgBognh8TECr0-R6R85sI07bcYZnBVPgbWv76IxJupbCgJTPqQEQ4g' alt='Taiwan President Lai Ching-te speaks during a news conference in Taipei in February 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Decision timing: Trump told reporters he would decide on the arms sale in a “fairly short period of time” after discussing the issue in detail with Xi.</li><li>Diplomatic follow-up: Taiwan’s Deputy Foreign Minister Chen Ming-chi said the government remains in constant contact with Washington over the pending arms sale.</li><li>Taiwan status: Lai said Taiwan is already a sovereign, independent democratic country, is not part of the People’s Republic of China, and that its future must follow the will of Taiwan’s people.</li><li>Military pressure: Taiwanese officials cited frequent Chinese warship and fighter jet activity around the island and accused Beijing of using intimidation to force what China calls reunification.</li></ul><blockquote>China has never abandoned its intention to annex Taiwan by force and continues to expand its military capabilities in an attempt to alter the regional and cross-strait status quo - Lai Ching-te</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer: Greer said Trump was focused on making sure “nothing happens there” and would keep his own counsel on whether and when to proceed with Taiwan arms sales. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/17/greer-trump-taiwan-arms-china-00925454'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Taiwan representative Alexander Yui: Yui told CBS that Taiwan wants peace and stability and said, “we’re not the ones creating all this trouble” with China. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taiwan-peace-stability-trouble-china-alexander-yui/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Chinese President Xi Jinping: Chinese state media reported that Xi told Trump there would be “conflicts” if the Taiwan situation was not handled properly. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/taiwans-representative-to-us-says-were-not-the-ones-creating-all-this-trouble-with-china/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Former U.S. Defense Secretary Robert Gates: Gates said it would be a mistake for Washington to change its carefully worded position on Taiwan after Trump said he and Xi had talked extensively about the issue. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/robert-gates-says-it-would-be-a-mistake-for-the-us-to-change-its-rhetoric-on-taiwan/'>CBS News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/17/taiwan-trump-weapons-sales-00925594'>Taiwan urges Trump to stay the course on weapons sales</a> - politico.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taiwan-peace-stability-trouble-china-alexander-yui/'>Taiwan representative to U.S.: &quot;We